==Finnish==

===Etymology===
{{com|fi|keski-|t1=mid-, middle|viikko|t2=week}}, a calque either of {{cal|notext=1|fi|gml|middewēke}} (compare modern {{cog|de|Mittwoch}}),<ref>{{R:fi:SES}}</ref> or of a seemingly unattested {{calque|notext=1|fi|gmq-osw|-}} word (compare {{ncog|non|miðvikudagr|lit=mid-week day}}).<ref>{{R:fi:SSA}}</ref> Note that Wednesday was the middle of the seven-day week when Sunday was still the first day of the week in Finland prior to 1973.

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-pronunciation|keski-viikko|a=Fi-keskiviikko.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{enum|fi|tiistai|torstai|day of the week|ke}}
{{fi-noun}}

# [[Wednesday]] {{gloss|day of the week; the third day of the week in Finland and in the ISO 8601 standard}}

====Usage notes====
As with all days of the week in Finnish, the essive case ({{m|fi||keskiviikkona}}) is usually used when talking about what will happen or happened on a Wednesday.

